al agents capable of inducing 双硫仑样反应. Evidence indicates that certain cephalosporins, including 头孢孟多, contain a methylthiotetrazole side chain that inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, thereby precipitating the reaction upon ethanol exposure. Similarly, nitroimidazoles—such as metronidazole—are well-documented triggers. Clinical literature confirms that these drug classes are among the most commonly reported causes of this adverse reaction. [Data: Entities (4924, 10587); Relationships (10936, 10172)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"乙醛 accumulation is the biochemical mechanism underlying the reaction\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The pathophysiology of 双硫仑样反应 is directly tied to elevated levels of 乙醛 (acetaldehyde), a toxic metabolite of ethanol. Normally, acetaldehyde is rapidly converted to acetate by aldehyde dehydrogenase; however, drugs like 头孢孟多 and 咪唑类抗生素 inhibit this enzyme, causing acetaldehyde to accumulate. This buildup produces the characteristic symptoms of the reaction. One study noted that in subjects experiencing the reaction after administration of cefotetan, only one exhibited a marked increase in blood acetaldehyde concentration, supporting the causal link. [Data: Entities (13302); Relationships (1248)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Respiratory and cardiovascular symptoms are prominent clinical features\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Multiple severe symptoms associated with 双硫仑样反应 involve the respiratory and cardiovascular systems. These include 胸闷 (chest tightness), 端坐呼吸 (orthopnea), 哮鸣音 (wheezing), and 喉中痰鸣 (audible phlegm in the throat). Documented case reports describe patients developing acute respiratory distress—characterized by increased respiratory rate, orthopnea, and diffuse wheezing—shortly after receiving ethanol-containing infusions (e.g., hydrocortisone injection) while on cephalosporin therapy. These manifestations can mimic asthma exacerbations or heart failure, complicating diagnosis. [Data: Entities (18418, 17959, 17961, 18322); Relationships (8864, 17873, 17874, 17904)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Neurological and systemic symptoms reflect reaction severity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond cardiopulmonary effects, 双硫仑样反应 can manifest with neurological and systemic signs such as 短暂失语 (transient aphasia), 不适 (general malaise), and 转氨酶一过性增高 (transient elevation of transaminases). In a review of 220 cases, transient aphasia and liver enzyme abnormalities were documented among less common but clinically significant presentations. Severe cases may involve cerebral edema and increased intracranial pressure, leading to temporary neurological deficits. These findings highlight the multisystem nature of the reaction and its potential for serious outcomes. [Data: Entities (17823, 18302, 18533); Relationships (17863, 17903, 17912)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Dermatological and autonomic signs are frequently observed\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Common early indicators of 双硫仑样反应 include 面色潮红 (facial flushing), 眼结膜充血 (conjunctival congestion), and 口鼻出气有酒精气味 (alcohol odor on breath). These autonomic and dermatological signs result from vasodilation and acetaldehyde toxicity. Facial flushing and conjunctival injection are consistently reported across multiple sources as hallmark features appearing within 15–30 minutes of ethanol exposure. The presence of an alcohol odor—even without recent beverage consumption—can be a critical diagnostic clue when ethanol-containing medications are administered. [Data: Entities (17921, 18292, 18293, 18564); Relationships (17868, 17901, 17902, 17914)]\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the potentially life-threatening nature of 双硫仑样反应, its association with commonly used antibiotics, and the risk of misdiagnosis leading to inappropriate treatment.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "